g the evening meal took 2 ____.”","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"hours"},{"id":"B","text":"hour"},{"id":"C","text":"hourly"},{"id":"D","text":"hour’s"}],"answerId":"A","hint":"This measurement stands **after the verb**, outside a compound modifier.","explanation":"After the verb took, the measurement is a normal plural noun phrase: **hours**.","keywords":["measure-after-noun"],"skill":"measure-after-noun","contextKey":"noun-modifiers-49","fingerprint":"f02a3718d9e17b97f01c2f5a66edec8c95978f327d66a7d0dc9c309d318fa061","source":"seed","provenance":{"kind":"original-guidance-aligned","referenceIds":["bc-noun-modifiers"],"version":"core-2026-09-v3","reviewStatus":"automated-checked; educator-review-pending"},"teaching":{"steps":["This measurement stands **after the verb**, outside a compound modifier.","After the verb took, the measurement is a normal plural noun phrase: **hours**.","Choose **hours**. Read the complete sentence again and check both its form and its intended meaning."],"choiceReasons":{"A":"After the verb took, the measurement is a normal plural noun phrase: **hours**.","B":"The number is greater than one, so the separate measure noun is plural.","C":"Hourly is an adjective/adverb, not the unit noun required here.","D":"A possessive apostrophe is not needed to state this duration."}}},{"id":"a8f8155b-32d9-5897-af74-6015b0647001","topicSlug":"noun-modifiers","level":"C1","prompt":"Choose the possessive duration: “We completed a ____ work on the evening meal.”","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"days"},{"id":"B","text":"day"},{"id":"C","text":"day’s"},{"id":"D","text":"days’"}],"answerId":"C","hint":"The article **a** specifies one day, and the phrase asks for its possessive form.","explanation":"A singular time unit in the possessive takes **’s**: a day’s work.","keywords":["one-day-possession"],"skill":"one-day-possession","contextKey":"noun-modifiers-49","fingerprint":"d2cb06b51c559be506939a4d0a2ff97be01c3b813fcd5d2f0d7e27f5ab9be107","source":"seed","provenance":{"kind":"original-guidance-aligned","referenceIds":["bc-noun-modifiers"],"version":"core-2026-09-v3","reviewStatus":"automated-checked; educator-review-pending"},"teaching":{"steps":["The article **a** specifies one day, and the phrase asks for its possessive form.","A singular time unit in the possessive takes **’s**: a day’s work.","Choose **day’s**. Read the complete sentence again and check both its form and its intended meaning."],"choiceReasons":{"A":"A plain plural lacks the possessive marking and conflicts with a.","B":"The requested duration phrase is possessive: a day’s work.","C":"A singular time unit in the possessive takes **’s**: a day’s work.","D":"Days’ is plural possession, but a specifies one day."}}},{"id":"7417ef39-639c-503c-ae33-3161442a90f2","topicSlug":"noun-modifiers","level":"C1","prompt":"“A decision was made to prepare the evening meal.” Which revision names the decision-maker clearly?","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"A decision about the evening meal was made."},{"id":"B","text":"The making of a decision to prepare the evening meal occurred."},{"id":"C","text":"It was decided to prepare the evening meal."},{"id":"D","text":"The committee decided to prepare the evening meal."}],"answerId":"D","hint":"Look for an explicit **actor as subject**, not just a noun naming the decision.","explanation":"The active verb and explicit subject **the committee** identify who made the decision.","keywords":["nominalisation-agent"],"skill":"nominalisation-agent","contextKey":"noun-modifiers-49","fingerprint":"f35c54080363302ccd65641d6dbba32d553d89ad543e2bcdee2dc5cc5fe361ff","source":"seed","provenance":{"kind":"original-guidance-aligned","referenceIds":["bc-noun-modifiers"],"version":"core-2026-09-v3","reviewStatus":"automated-checked; educator-review-pending"},"teaching":{"steps":["Look for an explicit **actor as subject**, not just a noun naming the decision.","The active verb and explicit subject **the committee** identify who made the decision.","Choose **The committee decided to prepare the evening meal.**. Read the complete sentence again and check both its form and its intended meaning."],"choiceReasons":{"A":"This still omits who made the decision.","B":"The nominalised wording still hides the decision-maker.","C":"This impersonal passive also omits the decision-maker.","D":"The active verb and explicit subject **the committee** identify who made the decision."}}},{"id":"172c608b-7351-5f78-a695-aaddd292a71e","topicSlug":"noun-modifiers","level":"C1","prompt":"Choose the modifier: “It is a ____ task to repair the rowing boat.”","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"3 hours''"},{"id":"B","text":"3-hourly"},{"id":"C","text":"3-hour"},{"id":"D","text":"3-hours"}],"answerId":"C","hint":"Before a noun, build **number + singular unit** as a compound modifier.","explanation":"A compound measurement before task uses a singular unit: **3-hour**.","keywords":["measure-compound"],"skill":"measure-compound","contextKey":"noun-modifiers-50","fingerprint":"805f9394352c5a8fb6c26a0922a53ae28908286de632af5aeab01acce6cb004d","source":"seed","provenance":{"kind":"original-guidance-aligned","referenceIds":["bc-noun-modifiers"],"version":"core-2026-09-v3","reviewStatus":"automated-checked; educator-review-pending"},"teaching":{"steps":["Before a noun, build **number + singular unit** as a compound modifier.","A compound measurement before task uses a singular unit: **3-hour**.","Choose **3-hour**. Read the complete sentence again and check both its form and its intended meaning."],"choiceReasons":{"A":"This is a possessive-shaped duration, not the adjective compound after a.","B":"Hourly means occurring each hour, not lasting this many hours.","C":"A compound measurement before task uses a singular unit: **3-hour**.","D":"The unit stays singular inside this compound modifier."}}},{"id":"6176257a-510e-50ef-ab25-467982a1e065","topicSlug":"noun-modifiers","level":"C1","prompt":"“The task of repairing the rowing boat took 3 ____.”","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"hour"},{"id":"B","text":"hourly"},{"id":"C","text":"hour’s"},{"id":"D","text":"hours"}],"answerId":"D","hint":"This measurement stands **after the verb**, outside a compound modifier.","explanation":"After the verb took, the measurement is a normal plural noun phrase: **hours**.","keywords":["measure-after-noun"],"skill":"measure-after-noun","contextKey":"noun-modifiers-50","fingerprint":"dd642c462326b8a07dbf525105ad9fcf7ae3b5a17d516fd58405bb88d3aef100","source":"seed","provenance":{"kind":"original-guidance-aligned","referenceIds":["bc-noun-modifiers"],"version":"core-2026-09-v3","reviewStatus":"automated-checked; educator-review-pending"},"teaching":{"steps":["This measurement stands **after the verb**, outside a compound modifier.","After the verb took, the measurement is a normal plural noun phrase: **hours**.","Choose **hours**. Read the complete sentence again and check both its form and its intended meaning."],"choiceReasons":{"A":"The number is greater than one, so the separate measure noun is plural.","B":"Hourly is an adjective/adverb, not the unit noun required here.","C":"A possessive apostrophe is not needed to state this duration.","D":"After the verb took, the measurement is a normal plural noun phrase: **hours**."}}},{"id":"82c4f06a-4fd4-509b-a064-b1d89e82d714","topicSlug":"noun-modifiers","level":"C1","prompt":"Choose the possessive duration: “We completed a ____ work on the rowing boat.”","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"day’s"},{"id":"B","text":"days’"},{"id":"C","text":"days"},{"id":"D","text":"day"}],"answerId":"A","hint":"The article **a** specifies one day, and the phrase asks for its possessive form.","explanation":"A singular time unit in the possessive takes **’s**: a day’s work.","keywords":["one-day-possession"],"skill":"one-day-possession","contextKey":"noun-modifiers-50","fingerprint":"bf49e9a3ee3b018dfdb40bb301a080301eba054df3bdfe7051ad32df93643e69","source":"seed","provenance":{"kind":"original-guidance-aligned","referenceIds":["bc-noun-modifiers"],"version":"core-2026-09-v3","reviewStatus":"automated-checked; educator-review-pending"},"teaching":{"steps":["The article **a** specifies one day, and the phrase asks for its possessive form.","A singular time unit in the possessive takes **’s**: a day’s work.","Choose **day’s**. Read the complete sentence again and check both its form and its intended meaning."],"choiceReasons":{"A":"A singular time unit in the possessive takes **’s**: a day’s work.","B":"Days’ is plural possession, but a specifies one day.","C":"A plain plural lacks the possessive marking and conflicts with a.","D":"The requested duration phrase is possessive: a day’s work."}}},{"id":"6af67193-d1e8-5605-a854-68cc87999ca6","topicSlug":"noun-modifiers","level":"C1","prompt":"“A decision was made to repair the rowing boat.” Which revision names the decision-maker clearly?","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"The committee decided to repair the rowing boat."},{"id":"B","text":"A decision about the rowing boat was made."},{"id":"C","text":"The making of a decision to repair the rowing boat occurred."},{"id":"D","text":"It was decided to repair the rowing boat."}],"answerId":"A","hint":"Look for an explicit **actor as subject**, not just a noun naming the decision.","explanation":"The active verb and explicit subject **the committee** identify who made the decision.","keywords":["nominalisation-agent"],"skill":"nominalisation-agent","contextKey":"noun-modifiers-50","fingerprint":"5dc5b0a931a89f6040d11a7cb26c4e6d5fce9268319d2d46c8f9b3f2461480c9","source":"seed","provenance":{"kind":"original-guidance-aligned","referenceIds":["bc-noun-modifiers"],"version":"core-2026-09-v3","reviewStatus":"automated-checked; educator-review-pending"},"teaching":{"steps":["Look for an explicit **actor as subject**, not just a noun naming the decision.","The active verb and explicit subject **the committee** identify who made the decision.","Choose **The committee decided to repair the rowing boat.**. Read the complete sentence again and check both its form and its intended meaning."],"choiceReasons":{"A":"The active verb and explicit subject **the committee** identify who made the decision.","B":"This still omits who made the decision.","C":"The nominalised wording still hides the decision-maker.","D":"This impersonal passive also omits the decision-maker."}}}],"chapter":{"introduction":["Build compact noun phrases and understand how turning actions into nouns changes a sentence’s focus.","The form tells you how to build the sentence; the context tells you why that form is useful. Read each section with both questions in mind. The examples make one contrast at a time, while the practice mixes the skills so that you learn to recognise the pattern yourself."],"rules":[{"title":"Nouns can modify other nouns","explanation":"A noun before another noun often classifies its purpose or type. The final noun is usually the head and controls number and agreement. The first noun often stays singular: a book shelf, two book shelves. Some established compounds keep a plural modifier.","pattern":"a **shoe shop** / two **shoe shops**","commonMistakes":["A sports centre is an established plural modifier. The singular-modifier pattern has lexical exceptions."],"examples":["Choose the modifier: “It is a **2-hour** task to prepare the visitor guide.”","Choose the modifier: “It is a **3-hour** task to restore the old portrait.”"],"skill":"form"},{"title":"Measure compounds before nouns","explanation":"A measurement used as a compound modifier usually has a singular unit and is often hyphenated. When the same measurement follows the noun as a separate phrase, the unit can be plural.","pattern":"a **three-hour** journey / a journey of **three hours**","commonMistakes":["A three-hours journey incorrectly pluralises the unit inside the usual compound modifier."],"examples":["“The task of preparing the visitor guide took 2 **hours**.”","“The task of restoring the old portrait took 3 **hours**.”"],"skill":"meaning"},{"title":"Possessive relationships and of phrases","explanation":"A possessive can express relationships beyond ownership, including time and association. Noun modifiers often classify; possessives often identify a particular relationship. An of phrase may be clearer with long or abstract noun phrases.","pattern":"a **day’s** work / the roof **of the building**","commonMistakes":["A children’s book and a child’s book can differ: the former can name a category, the latter a book belonging to one child."],"examples":["Choose the possessive duration: “We completed a **day’s** work on the visitor guide.”","Choose the possessive duration: “We completed a **day’s** work on the old portrait.”"],"skill":"contrast"},{"title":"Nominalisation and clarity","explanation":"Nominalisation turns an action or quality into a noun, often useful in academic writing: decide → decision, develop → development. It can make a process the subject of a sentence but can also hide who acted. Keep the agent explicit when responsibility matters.","pattern":"We decided quickly. → **Our decision** was quick.","commonMistakes":["Long strings of abstract nouns are not automatically clearer or more precise than verbs."],"examples":["“A decision was made to prepare the visitor guide.” Which revision names the decision-maker clearly? → **The committee decided to prepare the visitor guide.**","“A decision was made to restore the old portrait.” Which revision names the decision-maker clearly? → **The committee decided to restore the old portrait.**"],"skill":"application"}],"recap":["a **shoe shop** / two **shoe shops**","a **three-hour** journey / a journey of **three hours**","a **day’s** work / the roof **of the building**","We decided quickly. → **Our decision** was quick."]},"stage":"6 · Advanced control","prerequisites":["unreal-subjunctive"]}'::jsonb) ON CONFLICT(version,slug) DO NOTHING;
